/-!
# The ramification polygon of a local-field extension

For a polynomial `φ : 𝒪[K][X]` and an element `α : L` of a local-field extension `L/K`, the
**ramification polygon** `φ.ramificationPolygon α` is the Newton polygon of the ramification
polynomial `φ.ramificationPolynomial α` with respect to `TauCeti.normalizedAddValuation L`.

When `L/K` is separable and generated by a root `ξ : 𝒪[L]` of a monic Eisenstein polynomial
`φ : 𝒪[K][X]` of degree `n = e(L/K) = ε * p ^ w` (`p = ringChar 𝓀[K]`,
`w = wildRamificationExponent K L`):

* the polygon runs from `(1, ramificationHeight K L)` on the left to `(n, 0)` on the right, with
  `n - 1` finite unit slopes;
* over any normal (or splitting) local-field extension `M/L/K`, the finite Newton slopes of
  `φ.ramificationPolynomial (ξ : L)` are the negatives of the rational conjugate breaks
  `conjugateBreaks K L M (ξ : L)`;
* `conjugateBreaks K L M (ξ : L)` depends only on `L/K` and not on the choice of uniformizer `ξ`,
  so `φ.ramificationPolygon (ξ : L)` is independent of `(φ, ξ)`;
* the polygon equals `0` on the tame interval `[p ^ w, n]` (forming the finite face of slope `0`,
  so exactly `n - p ^ w` conjugate breaks vanish) and is strictly positive on `[1, p ^ w)`;
* on each prime-power block `[p ^ r, p ^ (r + 1))` (`r < w`), Kummer domination prevents interior
  contact with the coefficient heights, so the unit slope is constant on `[p ^ r, p ^ (r + 1))` and
  vertices can occur only at `{1, p, …, p ^ w, n}`;
* reading these `w` prime-power slopes from `p ^ w` down to `1` defines the rational list
  `φ.rams (ξ : L) : List ℚ` (`0 < r₁ ≤ ⋯ ≤ r_w`), its number of distinct positive slopes
  `φ.wildSegments (ξ : L) ≤ w`, the slope dictionary reconstructing `newtonSlopes`, the conjugate
  tail count `#{σ ≠ incl : v_L((σ ξ - ξ)/ξ) ≥ t} = p ^ (countP (t ≤ ·) rams) - 1` for `t > 0`, and
  the denominator divisibility `q.den ∣ count (-q) newtonSlopes`.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.conjugateBreaks K L M α`: the multiset of normalized conjugate-difference valuations
  `(v_M(σ α - α) - v_M(α)) / e(M/L) : ℚ` over `σ ∈ (L →ₐ[K] M) \ {incl}`.
* `Polynomial.ramificationPolygon φ α`: the Newton polygon of `φ.ramificationPolynomial α` with
  respect to `normalizedAddValuation L`.
* `Polynomial.ramificationPolygonRat φ α`: the rational height of `φ.ramificationPolygon α` at each
  index (`0` where the polygon is `⊤`).
* `Polynomial.rams φ α`: the list of `wildRamificationExponent K L` rational slopes of
  `φ.ramificationPolygon α` on `[p ^ (w - k), p ^ (w - k + 1)]`, ordered nondecreasingly.
* `Polynomial.wildSegments φ α`: `(φ.rams α).dedup.length`, the number of wild segments.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.IsTotallyRamified.conjugateBreaks_eq_of_irreducible`: independence of `conjugateBreaks`
  from the uniformizer.
* `Polynomial.newtonSlopes_ramificationPolynomial_eq_map_conjugateBreaks`: Newton slopes of the
  ramification polynomial are the negated conjugate breaks.
* `Polynomial.ramificationPolygon_eq_of_eisenstein_adjoin_eq_top`: independence of the ramification
  polygon from the monic Eisenstein polynomial and uniformizer.
* `Polynomial.isFiniteFace_ramificationPolygon_zero` and
  `TauCeti.IsTotallyRamified.card_filter_conjugateBreaks_eq_zero`: the tame flat face `[p ^ w, n]`
  and its zero-break count `n - p ^ w`.
* `Polynomial.unitSlope_ramificationPolygon_eq_of_mem_Ico`: vertices of the ramification polygon
  occur only at powers of `p` (and `n`).
* `Polynomial.pos_of_mem_rams`, `Polynomial.sortedLE_rams`,
  `Polynomial.newtonSlopes_ramificationPolynomial_eq_sum_rams`,
  `Polynomial.card_filter_conjugateBreaks_ge_of_pos`, and
  `Polynomial.den_dvd_count_newtonSlopes_ramificationPolynomial`: positivity, monotonicity, slope
  dictionary, conjugate-break tail count, and denominator divisibility of the rams.

## References

* Ø. Ore, *Newtonsche Polygone in der Theorie der algebraischen Körper*, Math. Ann. **99** (1928),
  84–117.
* [J.-P. Serre, *Corps Locaux*][serre1968], Chapter IV, §§1–2.
* C. Greve and S. Pauli, *Ramification polygons, splitting fields, and Galois groups of Eisenstein
  polynomials*, Int. J. Number Theory **8** (2012), 1401–1424.
-/

public section
noncomputable section

